# mm.led

A portable interface to a chain of addressable RGB LEDs: one LED is a chain of
one. Colour policy, animation, and brightness live above this module, and the
wire protocol below it, in a controller such as mm.led.ws2812b.

Its lifecycle is mm.display's. count says how many LEDs the chain has.
initialize claims the transport and leaves every LED off. write changes the
colours the controller holds for a run of LEDs, starting at first; refresh
sends them, which is when the LEDs change. clear turns every LED off and
refreshes, and sleep turns them off and returns the transport, after which
initialize may claim it again. A write before initialize, or after sleep, is
NotInitialized.

A Color is eight bits each of red, green, and blue, in that order whatever
order the LEDs take them on the wire: that is the controller's to know. The
values are what is sent, not what the eye sees -- the scale is not
perceptually even, and full white on a WS2812B is bright enough to be
uncomfortable at a desk -- so brightness is the caller's to apply.

The platform-interface marker makes a selected board LED provider part of an
executable only when its ordinary closure reaches mm.led. Host tests may
replace the linkable Unsupported fallback directly.
